<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abraham Roentgen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abraham Roentgen was a German cabinetmaker who set up a furniture workshop at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1750. Trained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, he made fine marquetry furniture with hidden mechanical fittings for princely clients. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-roentgen">David Roentgen</a> took over the business and made it the most celebrated cabinetmaking firm in 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-roentgen.jpg" alt="Abraham Roentgen" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Juncker (1751 - 1817), court painter to the count of Wied · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abraham_Roentgen_(portrait_by_Johannes_Juncker).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
